---INSTRUCTION---
Write a function to determine if two strings are special-equivalent. Two strings are considered special-equivalent if it is possible to transform one string into the other by performing any number of swaps where each swap is between two characters at even indices or between two characters at odd indices. This means that the relative order of characters at even positions can be rearranged arbitrarily, and similarly for characters at odd positions, independently.

The function should be named `special_equivalent?` and accept two strings as input, returning a boolean value.

If the two strings have different lengths, they cannot be special-equivalent, so return false immediately.

The implementation should have a time complexity of O(n log n) where n is the length of the strings.
---TEST---
